    "result_text": "The change is complete and the test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `namesakeCount` on the `POST /api/owners` create flow \u2014 the number of existing owners that already share the new owner's `firstName` and `lastName`, compared case-insensitively, counted before the new owner is saved.\n\nChanges:\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `namesakeCount` (int32, min 0) property to the `Owner` response schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it.\n- **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column field with getter/setter (persisted so `GET /api/owners/{id}` reads it back).\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 added a `namesakeCount(owner)` helper that counts existing owners with a case-insensitive first+last name match, and set it on the owner before saving.\n- **schema.sql** (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column. The tests run under hsqldb with `ddl-auto=none`, so the hsqldb column is required; the others were updated for consistency.\n\nMapStruct auto-maps `namesakeCount` from the entity to the DTO since both expose the property, so `GET` returns it without mapper changes. The two same-name owners in the test end up with `namesakeCount` = 1 on the second create, as asserted.",
